    "https://careers.callutheran.edu/postings/13659": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Music",
      "description": "Cal Lutheran Employment Opportunities | Assistant Professor of Music Skip to Main Content Careers at Cal Lutheran Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Applicant Interest Cards California Lutheran University Pacific Lutheran Theological Seminary Human Resources E-verify Employer Benefits Maps & Directions Student Employment Office Annual Security Report - All Campuses Assistant Professor of Music Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Position Information Position Title Assistant Professor of Music Position Category Faculty FLSA Exempt Position Status Tenure-Track FTE .75 (academic year full-time) Location Thousand Oaks University Background The mission of California Lutheran University is to educate leaders for a global society who are strong in character and judgment, confident in their identity and vocation, and committed to service and justice. Founded in 1959 in beautiful Ventura County, the university has an enrollment of about 3,500 undergraduate and graduate students who come from across the nation and around the world from a diversity of backgrounds, cultures and faiths. As a federally recognized Hispanic-Serving Institution, Cal Lutheran is looking for candidates with a dedication to and success in working in diverse communities. The university is committed to an inclusive learning and work environment that values individual differences and respects each person’s dignity. Basic Function Under the direction of the Dean and the Department Chair, the Assistant Professor is responsible for providing academic instruction to students for the assigned undergraduate courses. The Assistant Professor is also to serve as an academic mentor to students; serve on faculty or university committees; and engage in scholarly activities, and/or creative endeavors which contribute to the academic mission of the University, as outlined in the faculty handbook. The Music Department at California Lutheran University invites applicants for a tenure-track Assistant Professor whose primary responsibility will be to conduct the university choirs (including an all-student SATB mixed choir and treble choir), guide all vocal-related activities, and teach courses in the Music Department beginning in Fall 2027. The successful candidate will be a visionary, enthusiastic, and collaborative choral pedagogue and musician committed to building and maintaining a vibrant choral and vocal program. Proven or potential success with recruitment is expected. Teaching responsibilities include coursework in the candidate’s secondary areas of expertise and may include music theory, music history, conducting, private vocal lessons, or other subjects. Additional responsibilities involve the planning and performance of the annual Christmas Concerts. California Lutheran University is a Hispanic Serving Institution. We seek candidates with experience or sensitivity to working with students from underrepresented ethnic and racial backgrounds and educational attainment, including first-generation and/or immigrant students. The successful candidate will have a record of and a commitment to diversity, inclusion, equity, justice, and belonging, and who can serve as a mentor for African-American, Latina/o, and/or Native American students. The Music Department is a student-centered learning community that prides itself on providing a strong music education within a nurturing atmosphere. Faculty members are experienced professionals who are committed to teaching excellence. The music program is widely respected for its performance ensembles, its classroom teaching, and its studio instruction. Cal Lutheran’s majors have gone on to be successful performers, educators, composers, conductors and music-industry specialists. We embody the spirit of a strong conservatory education within a liberal arts setting. Representative Duties Primary responsibilities for this position include conducting the CLU Choir (an all-student SATB mixed choir), Cielo (treble choir) and overseeing all vocal-related activities within the department. Organize the annual University Christmas Concerts. E Proactively seek opportunities to recruit and retain students for the Music Department. E In coordination with the department chair and full-time faculty in the Music Department, plan and engage in domestic and international tours. E Additional teaching responsibilities will include coursework in the candidate’s secondary areas of expertise. This may include music theory, aural skills, music history, conducting, private vocal lessons, or other subjects. E Engage in service to the CLU Music Department and the University, particularly within the university’s ceremonial traditions that involve vocal groups (Christmas concerts, university convocations, baccalaureate, graduation, others). E Support music students by regularly attending performance forums, student recitals, and student ensemble performances. E Participate in departmental efforts to create meaningful curricular initiatives. E Willingly engage in service and leadership roles as requested by the Music Chair, Associate Dean, Dean, or other university leaders. E Provide academic and professional mentoring to students. E Provide academic instruction to students through lecture, lab, or performance courses. Create assignments and activities in one-on-one, classroom, and distance mediated instructional settings for development of cognitive abilities, communication skills, and higher order thinking skills among students. E Observe and evaluate student performance in meeting course objectives and student learning outcomes through assignments, projects, discussions, and examinations; provide feedback in a timely manner to student inquiries in class, online, or during established consultation office hours. E Maintain current, accurate records of course enrollment, attendance, student academic progress, and course curriculum; prepare and submit grades, data, and reports related to the course and student progress in a timely manner. E Maintain a program of scholarly and/or creative activity that is relevant to the area of choral music. E Teach and hold regular office hours in person on campus at least four days per week. E Provide a course syllabus to students in each class within the first week of the class, as outlined in the faculty handbook. E Adhere to the responsibilities related to teaching, advising, service, and scholarly, professional, and creative activities, as outlined in the faculty handbook. E Maintain professional and ethical standards in all duties E Attend and participate on committees and in the department, division, campus, and University meetings. Perform related duties as assigned. E Evening and weekend hours may be required. E E = Essential Duties Knowledge Of Current theory and practice within the discipline of choral conducting and singing. Appropriate repertoire for college-based choral ensembles. Effective pedagogical approaches to teaching students with diverse musical backgrounds and experiences. Experience and success working with a diverse student population, including students from historically underserved groups and first-generation students, as well as students with documented learning disabilities. Principles and best practices of effective teaching. Student learning outcomes and methods of assessment. University curricula. Community and campus resources, services, programs, and professionals available to students. Methods of gathering, analyzing, and preparing data Modern computing technology. Record-keeping techniques concerning student enrollment, attendance, and progress. Ability To Demonstrate a thorough and accurate knowledge of the subject, field, or discipline. Deliver course curricula in various learning styles. Exhibit sensitivity to and understanding of the diverse academic, social, economic, cultural, and ethnic backgrounds of University students. Exhibit sensitivity to and understanding of the needs of students with disabilities. Integrate current and emerging instructional delivery technologies into the learning process to enhance teaching and the educational experience. Maintain professional standards that protect student confidentiality. Work constructively with their colleagues and to treat faculty, staff, administrators, and students with respect and professionalism. Communicate effectively, both orally and in writing using tact, patience, and courtesy Establish and maintain effective working relationships. Analyze situations accurately and adopt effective courses of action. Demonstrate commitment to professional excellence and continuous growth within the subject, field, or discipline. Minimum Qualifications Possession of a terminal degree (D.M.A. or Ph.D.) in Choral Conducting, Vocal Performance, or Choral Music Education from an accredited institution. ABD will be considered with a set date for when the doctorate will be successfully earned. Preferred Qualifications Licenses and other requirements None Physical Abilities Detect to understand appropriate written correspondence; communicate clearly to exchange information in person and via the telephone or video; operate a computer and other office productivity machinery; stationary position for extended periods of time; move or transport light objects; position self to store and/or retrieve files. Working Environment Indoor classroom environment; subject to visiting a variety of classroom sites on campus. Hiring Range $73,000 - $82,000 Posting Detail Information Posting Number Faculty001332020 Number of Vacancies 1 Desired Start Date 08/23/2027 Position End Date Open Date 10/01/2026 Close Date 11/08/2026 Open Until Filled No Application Procedure/Special Instructions We look forward to reading your application. Please include the following materials with your application: 1. Cover Letter: Please tell us about yourself and explain how you meet the qualifications for the position. Please also describe how you plan to support the mission and Hispanic-Serving Institution designation of California Lutheran University. Two pages maximum. 2. Curriculum Vitae or Resume: Please provide a curriculum vitae or resume detailing your educational background, professional experience, teaching experience, publications, service, appointments or positions, honors, awards, and other relevant accomplishments. 3. Please include a list of professional references whom the University may contact. 4. Statement of Teaching: Please discuss your teaching philosophy and provide concrete examples of your pedagogy. Two pages maximum. 5. Statement of Research: Please describe your program of research/scholarly creativity, noting any work completed in collaboration with undergraduate students. If you have not previously conducted research/scholarly creativity with undergraduate students, please describe your plans for future scholarship and student collaboration. Two pages maximum. 6. Other – Inclusive Excellence Statement: Please explain how you will encourage and facilitate inclusive excellence through your teaching, mentorship, research, and service. Two pages maximum. Semifinalists candidates will be asked to submit videos of them conducting in a performance or rehearsal. Statement California Lutheran University is committed to providing equal opportunity in employment for all persons, regardless of race, religious creed, color, national origin, ancestry, physical disability, mental disability, medical condition, marital status, sex, age, sexual orientation, genetic information, gender, gender identity or expression, or any other basis protected by applicable law. No person shall be excluded from participation in, be denied the benefits of, or be subjected to discrimination in any program, activity or facility of the University on the basis of these factors in a manner consistent with applicable federal and state laws, regulations, ordinances, orders and rules, and University’s policies, procedures, and processes. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process can be provided to individuals with qualifying disabilities. Please contact the Human Resources Department for further information or to request an accommodation. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume/Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Teaching Philosophy Statement of Research/Creative Activities List of References Other Optional Documents Human Resources California Lutheran University 60 W. Olsen Rd. #1100 Thousand Oaks, CA 91360 Office (805) 493-3185 Fax (805) 493-3655 California Lutheran University is an Equal Opportunity Employer To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
